  "statement": "Merge all overlapping intervals and return an array of the non-overlapping intervals that cover them. Touching endpoints overlap. Sort the result by start time.\n",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= intervals.length <= 10^4",
    "0 <= start <= end <= 10^4"
  ],
  "function_name": "merge",
  "hints": [
    "Sorting by start time makes overlaps local. The next interval can only overlap the last merged one.",
    "If the next start is at most the last end, extend the last end to the maximum of the two ends.",
    "Otherwise append a new interval."
  ],
  "approaches": [
    {
      "name": "Compare all pairs",
      "complexity": {
        "time": "O(n^2)",
        "space": "O(n)"
      },
      "explanation": "Repeatedly merge any overlapping pair."
    },
    {
      "name": "Sort and sweep",
      "complexity": {
        "time": "O(n log n)",
        "space": "O(n)"
      },
      "explanation": "One pass after sorting."
    }
  ],
  "editorial": "Sort by start. The merged list's last interval is the only one that can still grow. An interval that starts after that end is disjoint and becomes a new group. An interval that starts earlier or at the end extends the group. The sort is the expensive step.",
  "reference_solution": "from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\nfrom collections import Counter, OrderedDict, defaultdict, deque\nimport heapq\n\ndef merge(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    ordered = sorted(intervals)\n    out = [ordered[0][:]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        if start <= out[-1][1]:\n            out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], end)\n        else:\n            out.append([start, end])\n    return out\n",
  "starter_code": "from typing import List\n\ndef merge(intervals: List[List[int]]) -> List[List[int]]:\n    pass\n",
